The class `RunTools` contains the two methods described below. In both methods, a *run* is a sequence of one or more identical consecutive characters.

```java
public class RunTools {
    /** Returns a String in which each run in s is replaced by the character followed by
     *  the length of the run. For example, compress("aaabcc") returns "a3b1c2".
     *  compress("") returns "". */
    public static String compress(String s)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Returns the length of the longest run in s, or 0 if s is empty.
     *  For example, longestRun("aaabccddddd") returns 5. */
    public static int longestRun(String s)
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `compress`.

**(b)** Write the method `longestRun`.

sampleResponse:
```java
public static String compress(String s) {
    String result = "";
    int i = 0;
    while (i < s.length()) {
        char c = s.charAt(i);
        int count = 0;
        while (i < s.length() && s.charAt(i) == c) {
            count++;
            i++;
        }
        result += c + "" + count;
    }
    return result;
}

public static int longestRun(String s) {
    int best = 0;
    int run = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
        if (i > 0 && s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(i - 1)) {
            run++;
        } else {
            run = 1;
        }
        if (run > best) {
            best = run;
        }
    }
    return best;
}
```
In `compress`, the inner loop's first test `i < s.length()` prevents an out-of-bounds access at the end of the string, and `c + "" + count` forces string concatenation.

